, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5466.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>Gandhar, Taluka Vagra, District Bharuch.</p><p>Main Deity: A white-colored image of Bhagwan Amizara Parshwanath in the padmasana (lotus) posture, approximately 175 centimeters tall.</p><p>Location: This temple stands by the seashore near Gandhar village.</p><p>History: Gandhar was an ancient and highly prosperous city in the medieval period. In those days it had 17 Jain temples. Remains of ancient Jain temples can still be found today. This city, once spread across an area of 3 miles, is today a very small village, famous for this Jain pilgrimage site. In ancient times Gandhar was a highly developed port and trading center. In 769-770 CE, the king of Sindh attacked Gandhar and destroyed all the temples and idols, converting most of the temples into mosques. In the 17th century the city again became prosperous and became a very popular Jain center with many Jain temples. Jagatguru Shri Hirvijaysurishwarji came to Gandhar with his vast community of disciples (about 300 monks and 700 nuns) for three consecutive Chaturmasas (four-month monsoon retreats). It was here that Emperor Akbar sent him an invitation to come to Fatehpur Sikri, which the revered Guru accepted. In the 18th century, when the city was being destroyed, most of the Tirthankara images were taken to the nearby Rajput state of Dahej village and buried underground there. Only the temple of Mahavir Swami and the temple of Amizara Parshwanath were spared, having been given the appearance of a mosque, with the images hidden in an underground chamber (bhoyaru); this chamber still exists today. The remaining temples were destroyed. In Vikram Samvat 1500, the temple of Mahavir Swami was built by Rajiya Vajiya of Khambhat.</p><p>This dilapidated temple was rebuilt in Vikram Samvat 1810, and the image of Shri Amizara Parshwanath was installed by the revered hands of Acharya Shri Vijayendrasurishwarji in Vikram Samvat 1659. This image was again installed in a newly built temple in Vikram Samvat 1964, on the fifth day of the bright half of the month of Magh. Since nectar (ami) is said to flow repeatedly from this image of Lord Parshwanath, the image came to be known as &quot;Amizara Parshwanath.&quot; Because this temple is located in Gandhar, this Parshwanath is also known as &quot;Gandharya Parshwanath.&quot; Acharyadev Shri Vijay Ramchandrasurishwarji was given diksha (monastic initiation) at this holy site in Gandhar in Vikram Samvat 1969.</p><p>Other Temples: Within the complex is a temple of Mahavir Swami, and there are three other temples here as well.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: The natural scenery of this place is very beautiful and enchanting. The temple walls bear paintings of pilgrimage sites such as Shatrunjay Mahatirth, Girnar, and Sammetshikhar. The image of Lord Parshwanath is ancient, beautiful, striking, miraculous and awe-inspiring. This is one of the five pilgrimage sites (Panchtirthi) of Bharuch.</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station is Pakhajan, 13 kilometers from the temple, on the Bharuch-Kavi railway line. This temple is 45 kilometers from Bharuch and 65 kilometers from Kavi. Bus service and private vehicles are available here. Dharamshala (pilgrim lodging) and bhojanshala (dining hall) facilities are available here, and there is also an upashraya.</p><p>Scripture References: Amizara Parshwanath is mentioned in &quot;365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Bhatev Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;108 Naam Garbhit Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;108 Naam Garbhit Shri Godi Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Hirvijay Suri Ras,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Naam Mala,&quot; &quot;Tirth Mala,&quot; &quot;135 Naamgarbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaitya Paripati,&quot; and others.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: O Lord who resides in Gandhar city, fragrant as sandalwood, from whose eyes and words nectar flows, O Parshwa Prabhu, seeing whom all afflictions and sorrows of devotees are extinguished -- &quot;I bow with devotion to Shri Amizara Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Amizara Parshwanathay Namah.&quot;</p><p>Temples of Shri Amizara Parshwanath exist at many places across India -- the main temple is in Gandhar. There are also temples at Khambhat, Dua (Banaskantha), near Jiralapado near Kheda, Sanand, Girnar Tirth, Aspur (Vagad) in Dungarpur district, Junnar, Patel Wada in Sangli (Maharashtra), Giriyadhar (Saurashtra), Shri Shatrunjay Tirth, Amijara (Madhya Pradesh), Kolivada Naka in Una, Mundra (Kutch), Vadali, Sojitra, and many other places. An image of Amizara Parshwanath is also found at the Jiravala Parshwanath pilgrimage site, at the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai, and at the Kesariyanagar temple in Palitana.</p><p>Trust: Shri Amizara Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Shri Gandhar Jain Derasar Trust, Mu. Po. Gandhar-392140, Taluka Vagra, District Bharuch, State Gujarat, India. Phone: 02641-232345, Mobile: 08200407999 (Jain Derasar Trust), 09909415904.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-004\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Kalhara Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-007\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Amizhara Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div cla